The #993 fix now has an end-to-end test, and the test fails when the fix is broken. It's committed as bbe333f2, a follow-up to 4c6fb486. Build, cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and cargo fmt --check all pass.

What the new test does: it runs the real binary with -p against a stub server that streams some text and then stalls. It waits until that text appears on stdout (no fixed sleep), then sends kill -INT.

After the fix:
• stdout is exactly "PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n", the streamed text with nothing added;
• stderr ends with (interrupted) and does not say "again";
• the exit code is 130.

A second test runs the same text with no signal: stdout is unchanged and the exit code is 0.

Checking that the test can fail: I broke the fix two ways, one at a time, restoring the code after each:
1. Sending the note through the REPL path made the test fail with the original bug's exact output on stdout: "PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n\n\n  (interrupted — press Ctrl+C again to exit)\n".
2. Ignoring the interrupt when choosing the exit code made it fail with exit code 0, as before the fix.

Docs: docs/src/usage/single-prompt.md now says that Ctrl+C exits 130, puts (interrupted) on stderr, and leaves already-streamed text on stdout.

Still not covered:
• **`--print` has no Ctrl+C test.** In that mode nothing is printed to stdout until the end, so the test has no reliable moment to send the signal. It uses the same interrupt handling and exit-code logic as plain -p, so it should behave the same, but no test exercises it.
• **The possible lost text from the first commit is still unchecked.** When Ctrl+C arrives, text from an unfinished line that is still held in yoyo's output buffer may never reach stdout. The new test's streamed text ends in a newline, so it never hits that case.
